This is an altered version of Echo's Z80 program.

prog-z80.bin and prog-z80-pal.bin are built/prog-z80.bin and
built/prog-z80-pal.bin from Echo 1.68a-dr.1 (github.com/drojaazu/echo, tag
1.68a-dr.1), a fork of Echo 1.68a (github.com/sikthehedgehog/echo, commit
e326c87) with these changes:

1. Echo comes in two builds, tuned for NTSC and for PAL machines. Its note
   tables (FMFreqTable and PSGFreqTable in src-z80/player/freq.z80, Z80
   address $1200-$12A7) hold:

   prog-z80.bin (NTSC): Echo's own FM table, and PSG periods for the NTSC
   PSG clock (3579545 Hz) with A4 = 440 Hz, rounded to the nearest value.

   prog-z80-pal.bin (PAL): Echo's FM table scaled by the ratio of the PAL and
   NTSC clocks, and PSG periods for the PAL PSG clock (3546895 Hz).

   Echo's original has one build, with an NTSC FM table and a PSG table
   between the NTSC and PAL clocks whose upper octaves are rounded down. It
   plays PSG notes about 10 cents sharp of Echo's own FM notes on either
   machine type, and up to 119 cents sharp at the top. The PAL values are
   calculated from the PAL clocks and tested in emulators only.

2. UpdatePSG (src-z80/player/psg.z80) keeps a PSG instrument's semitone
   shift inside the frequency table, holding the note at C-3 or B-8. The
   original reads past either end of the table and sends those bytes to the
   PSG, which can change another channel's volume or silence the noise
   channel.

Built with z80asm 1.8. Outside the note tables, the two programs are
identical. The second change adds 11 bytes of code, taken from the padding
before the page-aligned tables, so code after it moves and the tables do not.

